<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding: 5px; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Family and I</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> She Is My Mom!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Show comprehension of basic expressions for greeting and leave-taking if supported by gestures. Interactive: Answer simple questions about oneself, using gestures or formulaic expressions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can listen for and understand words related to greetings, leave-takings, and family members (e.g., "hello," "goodbye," "mom," "dad"). Can follow instructions about family-related activities (e.g., "Find a picture of your family."). Can respond to questions about family members, using gestures or single words (e.g., "Who is he?" "Dad").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with "Hello, everyone!" and leads a "Hello Song" with simple gestures. Then, the teacher shows colorful flashcards of "mom," "dad," "brother," and "sister," asking students to point to their own family members if they have pictures or just to the air. (2-3 minutes)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up the "mom" flashcard and says clearly, "Mom. This is my mom. She is my mom." The teacher uses a pointing gesture towards the flashcard and then towards a photo of their own family (if appropriate) or a generic picture of a mom. The teacher emphasizes the /m/ sound in "mom" and then repeats with "dad," emphasizing the /d/ sound. The teacher models a simple greeting: "Hello, mom!" and wave. (3-4 minutes)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the "mom" flashcard: "Is this a house or a mom?" (Expected student response: "mom" or pointing to "mom").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows two flashcards (e.g., "ball" and "dad"): "Who is this? Is it a ball or a dad?" (Expected student response: "dad" or pointing to "dad").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Family and I".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue or teacher-narrated story with accompanying picture cards/flashcards (e.g., "Hello, Mom! Hello, Dad!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Introducing family members: Use simple sentences to describe family (e.g., "She is my mom."). Sociolinguistic: Describing Family Roles: Using appropriate language to describe family members and their roles (e.g., "This is my mother.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening Focus):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays a short audio clip or narrates a simple story (e.g., "This is Maria. She has a mom and a dad. Maria says 'Hello, Mom!' and 'Hello, Dad!'").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Teacher asks, "Who is in the story? A dog or a family?" (Students point to family pictures or say "family").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Teacher holds up flashcards of "mom" and "dad." After listening, teacher asks, "Who says 'Hello, Mom!' in the story?" (Students point to the child picture or respond with a gesture). Teacher asks, "Who is 'Dad'?" (Students point to the 'dad' flashcard).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Personal pronouns + verb ("She is," "He is"), Possessive adjectives ("my mom").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> mom, dad, brother, sister.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /m/ in "mom," /d/ in "dad," phonemic awareness /b/ in "brother," /s/ in "sister."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point (Controlled practice):</b> The teacher lays out flashcards for "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister" on the table. The teacher says a word (e.g., "mom"), and students listen and point to the correct flashcard. Repeat for all target words. The teacher then says a greeting, "Hello, mom!", and students listen and point to "mom."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Circle (Semi-controlled substitution drill - receptive):</b> Students receive a worksheet with small pictures of "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister." The teacher says, "Circle mom." Students listen and circle the picture of the mom. Repeat for "dad," "brother" (focus on /b/ sound), and "sister" (focus on /s/ sound). This checks listening comprehension of distinct words and phonemic awareness.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Gesture (Pair/Group Interaction - receptive):</b> In small groups or pairs, students are given a set of the same flashcards. One student is designated as the "listener." The teacher says a phrase like "She is my mom" or "He is my dad." The "listener" points to the correct flashcard. Students then switch roles after a few turns. The teacher monitors for correct listening and pointing.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Listen and Draw/Color. This focuses on receptive listening and demonstrates comprehension through a non-verbal output suitable for 1st grade.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives each student a worksheet with outlines of different family members (e.g., a blank figure for mom, dad, brother, sister) or a drawing of a family with some members missing details.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives simple, clear instructions focusing on listening comprehension:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Color the mom red."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Draw hair on the dad."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Find the brother. Color his shirt blue."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Hello, sister! Draw a smile on the sister."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher repeats instructions slowly and clearly, using gestures where necessary, allowing students time to process and perform the action.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This activity directly connects to the theme "She Is My Mom!" and scenario "My Family and I" by requiring students to understand instructions about specific family members.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and simple Product Assessment (worksheet completion).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete the "Listen and Draw/Color" worksheet. The teacher observes students during the activity for their ability to follow instructions related to family members and basic greetings (e.g., responding to "Hello, mom!" by pointing or acknowledging).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Following Instructions:</b> Student accurately colors/draws the correct family member as instructed (e.g., colors mom red, draws hair on dad).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Student responds appropriately to basic greetings and questions about family members (e.g., points to "mom" when asked "Who is she?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Recognition:</b> Student demonstrates recognition of "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister" through actions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher asks students:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you understand 'mom' and 'dad' today?" (Students show thumbs up or thumbs down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Draw a happy face if you can find your mom's picture when I say 'mom'."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you hear about family today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to understand the words?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Overall, students showed good receptive comprehension of "mom" and "dad" through pointing and coloring activities. The TPR and flashcards were effective for engaging 1st graders. Some students struggled with differentiating "brother" and "sister" initially, indicating a need for more focused phonemic awareness practice (/b/ vs /s/) in subsequent lessons. The coloring activity provided concrete evidence of listening comprehension. For the next lesson, I will provide more scaffolding for distinguishing "brother" and "sister" visually and auditorily.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core family vocabulary (mom, dad, brother, sister) and basic greetings through listening. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will revisit these same vocabulary words in a simple illustrated text, allowing students to visually connect the words they heard today with their written form, thereby building on their receptive understanding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>